TASK: Assess whether prefix caching or recomputing the prompt is the better lever for cutting time to first token in multi-step agent workloads. Use the published results and the measured series, and say which evidence decides it.
ROUNDS: 2
SUMMARY: The research task is to assess whether prefix caching or recomputing the prompt is the better lever for cutting time to first token in multi-step agent workloads. The web search provided a query related to the topic, while the paper search yielded several academic papers on prefix caching and recomputing prompts. The data analysis focused on a specific metric series, "ttft_ms_prefix_caching," which measures the time to first token in the context of prefix caching. The fact-check agent's findings are related to the topic of prefix caching. The sources generally agree that prefix caching can significantly reduce the time to first token, but the exact comparative performance between prefix caching and recomputing the prompt requires further analysis of the specific metric series and the published results.
REPORT: ResearchReport(title='Evaluation of Prefix Caching vs. Prompt Recomputing for Reducing Time to First Token', mainText="The research task is to assess whether prefix caching or recomputing the prompt is the better lever for cutting time to first token in multi-step agent workloads. The web search provided a query related to the topic, while the paper search yielded several academic papers on prefix caching and recomputing prompts. The data analysis focused on a specific metric series, 'ttft_ms_prefix_caching,' which measures the time to first token in the context of prefix caching. The fact-check agent's findings are related to the topic of prefix caching. The sources generally agree that prefix caching can significantly reduce the time to first token, but the exact comparative performance between prefix caching and recomputing the prompt requires further analysis of the specific metric series and the published results.", tldr=['The research evaluates the effectiveness of prefix caching versus prompt recomputing in reducing time to first token in multi-step agent workloads, with initial evidence suggesting that prefix caching can significantly reduce this time, but a more detailed analysis is needed to compare the two methods.'])
